General setup:

To get an up-to-date source, do

    setenv CVSROOT /afs/pppl.gov/common/cvs
    cd
    cvs co degas2

This will put the sources in ~/degas2.  (It's not essential that the
sources be in this location.  But this is what the Makefile assumes)

src/.emacs contains a couple of items to consider putting in ~/.emacs.

To get woven documentation:

    mkdir ~/degas2/tex
    cd ~/degas2/tex
    cp ../src/Makefile .
    gmake geometry.print

To compile the code on a Sun:

    mkdir ~/degas2/SUN
    cd ~/degas2/SUN
    cp ../src/Makefile .
    gmake randomtest

To compile the code for the Crays (or HP):

    (On Sun)
    mkdir <some-afs-directory>/degas2/CRAY
    ln -s <same-afs-directory>/degas2/CRAY ~/degas2/CRAY
    cd ~/src/CRAY
    cp ~/degas2/src/Makefile .
    gmake randomtest

    (On Cray)
    ln -s <same-afs-directory>/degas2 ~/degas2
    cd <some-work-directory>/CRAY
    cp ~/degas2/CRAY/Makefile .
    gmake randomtest

To compile the code for CCF:

    (On Sun)
    mkdir ~/degas2/SGI
    cd ~/degas2/SGI
    cp ../src/Makefile .
    gmake randomtest

    (On CCF)
    ln -s /n/lyman/u/<username>/degas2 ~/degas2
    cd ~/degas2/SGI
    gmake randomtest



Description of the programs

etags: a local version of the program to produce tags.

sysdeptest: a test of the various system dependent routines.

reactionwrite: converts cross-section data to REACTION.nc

datasetup: converts elements.input to elements.nc
	converts species.input to species.nc
	converts reactions.input to reactions.nc

problemsetup: converts problem.input and reaction data files to problem.nc

reactionwrite: converts some old Degas input files to netcdf format (to be
superceded by ratecalc).

pmiwrite: converts some old Degas surface data files into netcdf format.

readgeometry: reads a geometry specification file (specified as arg) to
creates geometry.nc

readbackground: reads background plasma data from degas input file (specified as arg) to
creates background.nc

flighttest: runs flights through the plasma (arg is the number of flights)
-- the prototype for degas 2.

randomtest: a simple test of the random number generator.  A good thing to
try on new architechures.

degasgeom: read a degas input file (as arg) and write out geometry.nc.

geomtesta: a program to plot slices through the geometry.  Reads a file
geometry.inp to determine the slice to plot.  Creates an HDF file
geomtesta.hdf.

readgeometry: read in a text file to specify the geometry.

Reference Materials:

CVS

   CVS (Concurrent Version System) is used to coordinate work on a code 
by a number of authors. CVS maintains a central, "safe" version of the
code; authors check out copies in their own disk space in order to
work on the code. By doing a cvs update, a given author can update his
local sources to incorporate changes others have saved to the central
repository. The cvs commit command allows him to save his own changes
to the repository.
   The cvs manual page contains all of the detailed commands; the page for
rcsintro may also be helpful as an introduction. Many cvs commands may
be executed from within Emacs. See the Emacs Info page on Pcl-cvs for 
more details.

FWEB

   The FWEB package provides a tool for code documentation as well as a 
powerful preprocessor. The typesetting abilities of FWEB allow authors
to insert TeX (or LaTeX) comments into their code. In DEGAS 2, the
preprocessor is utilized extensively for writing macros which allow
the code seen by the user to be significantly more powerful than pure
FORTRAN 77. For examples of the overall structure of FWEB programs, see
the *.web files; most of the macros are located in the header *.hweb files.
   FWEB is fully documented in the Info facility of Emacs.

netCDF

   netCDF provides a device-independent binary file format. Furthermore,
these files are self-describing. Most of the effort required to read
and write these files is handled by macros. These are documented in
the file netcdf.hweb. By adding the commands in src/.emacs to your
.emacs file, Emacs will be able to convert automatically these files 
to text and back to binary for perusal and editing.
   netCDF is fully documented in the Info facility of Emacs.
